  "primerVowelsBody": "Lies a, e, i, o und u klar und gleichmäßig. Die Hinweise zeigen eine praktische Annäherung; die Großbuchstaben markieren die Hauptbetonung.",
  "primerVowelGuide": "Hinweis: a klingt ungefähr wie «a», e wie «e», i wie «i», o wie «o», u wie «u».",
  "primerSilentHTitle": "h wird geschrieben, aber nicht gesprochen",
  "primerSilentHBody": "Im Spanischen hat h normalerweise keinen eigenen Laut. Das siehst du in hola und hasta luego.",
  "primerLlYTitle": "ll and y in early course words",
  "primerLlYBody": "ll is a single written unit in llamo and llamas. y is a separate letter, as in y tú. Regional pronunciation varies, but the written patterns remain visible.",
  "primerEnyeTitle": "ñ is a distinct Spanish letter",
  "primerEnyeBody": "ñ is not the same as n. Recognize it in España and keep the tilde as part of the Spanish spelling.",
  "primerRRTitle": "r and rr",
  "primerRRBody": "r and rr mark different written patterns. A single r appears in Madrid; rr is the strong pattern in perro. The Primer only builds recognition, not accent training.",
  "primerContextTitle": "Context patterns: c, g, qu, gu and gü",
  "primerContextBody": "c and g change their reading with the following vowel. qu commonly keeps the k-like reading before e/i; gu and gü show different written cues. Meet these patterns as complete words, not as a long rule table.",
  "primerAccentsTitle": "Accents and Spanish punctuation",
  "primerAccentsBody": "á, é, í, ó and ú are familiar vowels with a meaningful written accent. Notice the mark in Cómo and días; full Spanish stress rules are outside this Primer.",
